//! The longer routines, read as the same kind of tree.
//!
//! 4166 of them: the companion to [`crate::nested_routines`], which stopped at
//! routines of about forty lines. These run to a hundred and ten, up to thirty
//! steps, and as deep as 13 branches inside one another. They are read by the
//! same walk and recorded in the same shape, so the two tables can be compared
//! directly — and comparing them is the point of keeping them apart.
//!
//! Longer routines nest more. 2611 of the 4166 here have something inside something
//! else, against about two in five of the shorter ones. That is not a fact
//! about the decompiler; it is what the extra length is spent on.
//!
//! What does not change is where the work sits. A step at the outermost level
//! calls about one and three fifths things; one level in, about one; two in,
//! about five sixths; three in, about two thirds. The same fall as in the
//! shorter routines, from a higher start. Depth narrows conditions at every
//! length of routine.
//!
//! And 259 of them are constructors — the run time's make-an-instance with its
//! after-it-is-made — which again sit in different steps in **every single
//! one**. That rule has now held without exception across three separate
//! populations swept three different ways.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
#![allow(clippy::unreadable_literal)]

